docs/dump-hierarchy.md:21:`grantiva hierarchy` finds the session that `--keep-alive` published, opens an HTTP read against the running GrantivaAgent on its allocated port, and prints the XCUI accessibility tree. The session lives in `/tmp/grantiva-sessions/`: grantiva-runner writes `<pid>-<timestamp>.grantiva` (port and session ID) when it starts holding the session, and grantiva writes `<pid>.owner.json` next to it the moment the runner is spawned, recording which simulator UDID (or adb serial) that runner owns, plus `projectDirectory` (the directory the run started in, absolute, with symlinks resolved as realpath(3) resolves them: a run started in `/tmp/x` records `/private/tmp/x`), `platform` (`ios` or `android`), and `detached` (`true` only for `runner start --detach`). The MCP server uses a keep-alive session only when both match its own project directory and platform; both sides are canonicalised the same way, so `/tmp/x` and `/private/tmp/x` match (sidecars written by 2.1.0 with the `/tmp` spelling still match), while other differences, including case, do not. Both files are removed when the run ends; a file whose runner `pid` is no longer running is ignored, and so is a `run --keep-alive` session whose grantiva process is gone, so a crashed or `kill -9`'d run never masquerades as a live session. Its runner does not outlive it either: grantiva binds the runner's process group to its own lifetime, so when the grantiva process dies, however it dies, the runner is interrupted and releases the device within seconds. A `runner start --detach` session is the exception by design: it outlives the command that started it until `grantiva runner stop`.
docs/android.md:35:`grantiva-android.yml` may instead be written in Maestro format, as `grantiva.yml` can on iOS
